I have, for what feels like the last 15 years, always been surprised when I see someone in their
twenties stop dead in their tracks when I ask them to compress a folder so that they can email it to me. 
It happened once while I was attending college in Houston. Then it happened again, and again and again. 

The tasks were simple, I thought. 

"Did you save it in the cloud or locally to your machine?"
"Uh, I saved it on the internet."

"Did you click 'Save As' when you saved your PowerPoint?"
"Why does that matter?"

I don't think I'm that smart. I want to be abundantly clear. Curious? Absolutely. 
I just like learning, but that does not and never will mean that I'm smart. 

That is not the goal of why I'm writing this. 
In fact, I think that these observations scare me and should scare you too.

We used the landline to hear our loved ones voices for a period of time. We could put our ear to that piece of elongated
plastic so that the sweet tones of our family members could be heard half way across the world. A beautiful thing indeed.
The next iteration of that was finding ways to communicate with these new machines called computers. These big boxes with
pieces of electrical hardware that were encased in large manilla crates. We could finally store names and numbers and all sorts
of data digitally without using paper. What a treat that was. 

Then came the tidal wave: the mobile phone. Suddenly we weren't tethered to the confines of our own homes. We could
freely move about and engage with others while we commuted. We were hooked. Like a drug that you can't seem to get your mind off of (okay
I'll assume some of you have never experienced that and honestly, good for you.)

Social media networks and big large corporations and boy geniuses constantly vying for our attention. The mobile phone was a hit.

In the process of this rapid transformation, we forgot to slow down. We forgot to pay attention and that, ultimately, was the point.

I believe that it is a fundamental right of every human being to understand what they are consuming. Whether that be food, technology or 
any other artifically crafted good. You are entitled to know how it's made, who made it and why it was created in the first place. If that
information is not available, then it is within our power as responsible citizens to obtain it by any means necessary. 
I want to be clear: information is like water. It should never be withheld. 

The world I wish existed, would provide all of this on a silver platter. It would be fed to you like the adverts you get while
scrolling instagram. Instead, you're flooded with abstractions so far removed from the thing you're buying that you are sold on the 
lie of consumption bringing you bliss and happiness. Notice the word abstraction here because it's extremely important for understanding the points
I am attempting to make. 
